---
import DeckLayout from '../../layouts/DeckLayout.astro';
import Slide from '../../components/slides/Slide.astro';
import Step from '../../components/slides/Step.astro';
import Card from '../../components/slides/Card.astro';
import KeyTerms from '../../components/slides/KeyTerms.astro';
import Table from '../../components/slides/Table.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/slides/CodeBlock.astro';
import SpeakerNotes from '../../components/slides/SpeakerNotes.astro';

const nodeHashingCode = `class HashRing {
  constructor(replicas = 3) {
    this.replicas = replicas;
    this.ring = new Map();
    this.keys = [];
  }

  addNode(node) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.replicas; i++) {
      const hash = this._hash(\`\${node}:\${i}\`);
      this.ring.set(hash, node);
      this.keys.push(hash);
    }
    this.keys.sort((a, b) => a - b);
  }

  getNode(key) {
    if (this.ring.size === 0) return null;
    const hash = this._hash(key);
    const target = this.keys.find(k => k >= hash) || this.keys[0];
    return this.ring.get(target);
  }

  _hash(str) {
    let hash = 5381;
    for (let i = 0; i < str.length; i++) {
      hash = ((hash << 5) + hash) + str.charCodeAt(i);
    }
    return Math.abs(hash % 10000);
  }
}

const ring = new HashRing(3);
ring.addNode("node-alpha");
ring.addNode("node-beta");
console.log("Assigned 'session-10492' to:", ring.getNode("session-10492"));`;
---

<DeckLayout
  title="CS 4400: Distributed Systems Architecture"
  description="Complete teaching lecture notes for Distributed Systems Architecture"
>
  <!-- Slide 1: Title & Course Introduction -->
  <Slide
    id="intro"
    eyebrow="01 · DISTRIBUTED SYSTEMS ARCHITECTURE"
    title="Data Partitioning & Consistent Hashing"
    subtitle="Lecture 04 · Scalable Distributed State Management"
    unit="CS 4400: Distributed Systems"
  >
    <div class="space-y-4 max-w-4xl">
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        In traditional database clustering, partitioning keys across N servers using naive modulo hashing (hash(key) mod N) causes massive data movement whenever a node joins or fails: almost every key relocates to a different server ((N-1)/N ≈ 90–99% of all data).
      </p>
      <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
        <strong>Consistent Hashing</strong> solves this by mapping both servers and data keys onto a continuous 360° circular coordinate space (the hash ring). When a node leaves or joins the cluster, only K/N keys need to be remapped to the immediate successor node, leaving all other node assignments completely undisturbed.
      </p>
      <div class="p-4 rounded-lg bg-indigo-50 border-2 border-indigo-200 mt-4">
        <h4 class="text-xs font-bold text-indigo-950 uppercase tracking-wide mb-1">Learning Objective</h4>
        <p class="text-sm text-indigo-900 m-0 font-semibold">
          Understand hash ring topology, virtual replica factor calculation, node departure redistribution, and why consistent hashing forms the backbone of systems like Amazon Dynamo, Apache Cassandra, and Akamai CDNs.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Emphasize the difference between naive modulo partitioning and ring partitioning. Walk students through why naive modulo breaks cache hit rates when cluster size fluctuates.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 2: Template A — Main Column + Key Terms Sidebar -->
  <Slide
    id="mechanism"
    eyebrow="01 · DATA PARTITIONING"
    title="Hash Ring Topology & Successor Routing"
    layout="split-sidebar"
    unit="CS 4400: Distributed Systems"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-6 h-full">
      <!-- Main Content (2 Columns) -->
      <div class="md:col-span-2 space-y-4">
        <p class="text-base leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          The continuous integer hash space (typically 0 to 2^32 - 1 or 0 to 2^128 - 1) is treated as a logical ring where the maximum integer wraps around to zero. Both physical node identifiers (IP addresses, UUIDs) and data keys are passed through the same deterministic cryptographic hash function (such as MD5, SHA-256, or Murmur3).
        </p>
        
        <div class="lecture-card border-2 border-slate-300">
          <h4 class="text-sm font-bold text-slate-950 mb-2 uppercase">Successor Lookup Rule</h4>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 m-0 leading-relaxed font-medium">
            To locate which physical node owns key k, compute h = hash(k) and traverse the ring clockwise until reaching the first node whose position is greater than or equal to h. If h is greater than all nodes, the key wraps around to the very first node on the ring.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-slate-800 font-medium">
          <strong>Node Departure Invariant:</strong> When node $N_B$ crashes, its keys transfer exclusively to its immediate clockwise successor $N_C$. No other node's partition boundaries or stored objects are modified.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Key Terms Sidebar (1 Column) -->
      <div class="md:col-span-1">
        <KeyTerms
          title="Key Terms"
          terms={[
            {
              term: "Hash Ring",
              definition: "A circular hash domain [0, 2^n - 1] where values wrap continuously from maximum back to zero."
            },
            {
              term: "Successor Node",
              definition: "The first node encountered when traversing the hash ring clockwise from a key's hashed location."
            },
            {
              term: "Virtual Replicas (Vnodes)",
              definition: "Multiple virtual points assigned to each physical node to ensure uniform statistical distribution across the ring."
            }
          ]}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Explain clockwise traversal. Point out that without virtual nodes, physical node clustering can create hot spots where one server receives 4x more traffic.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 3: Template B — Dynamic Card Grid -->
  <Slide
    id="cap-tradeoffs"
    eyebrow="02 · DISTRIBUTED GUARANTEES"
    title="CAP Theorem: Fundamental Trade-offs"
    layout="card-grid"
    unit="CS 4400: Distributed Systems"
  >
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-3 gap-5 my-auto">
      <Card title="Consistency (CP)" badge="Linearizability" glow="indigo">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Rule: Reject writes during network partitions rather than serve stale data.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Every client read receives the most recent write or an error. When partitions occur, minority partition nodes reject updates to prevent divergence. Examples: Apache HBase, Google Spanner, etcd.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Availability (AP)" badge="Eventual Consistency" glow="cyan">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Rule: Accept every read/write even if node synchronization is broken.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Every non-failing node returns a non-error response, with no guarantee that it contains the absolute latest write. Conflict resolution occurs asynchronously via vector clocks or CRDTs. Examples: Cassandra, DynamoDB.
        </p>
      </Card>

      <Card title="Partition Tolerance (P)" badge="Hard Reality" glow="rose">
        <p class="text-xs text-slate-700 font-bold mb-2">Rule: Network communication between physical servers will inevitably fail.</p>
        <p class="text-xs leading-relaxed text-slate-900 font-medium">
          Packet loss, hardware switches rebooting, and datacenter fiber cuts mean partitions cannot be avoided in real systems. Architects must choose between Consistency (CP) or Availability (AP) during partitions.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Clarify common misconception: you don't pick 2 out of 3. Networks will partition (P is fixed), so you choose how to behave when P happens (C vs A).
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 4: Template C — High-Contrast Comparison Table -->
  <Slide
    id="consensus-comparison"
    eyebrow="03 · DISTRIBUTED CONSENSUS"
    title="Consensus Protocol Comparison"
    layout="table"
    unit="CS 4400: Distributed Systems"
  >
    <div class="my-auto">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-medium mb-3">
        Comparing protocol guarantees, quorum fault tolerance thresholds, and operational trade-offs across common distributed consensus algorithms:
      </p>
      
      <Table
        headers={["Protocol", "Fault Model", "Quorum Requirement", "Leader Type", "Primary Industrial Use Case"]}
        rows={[
          ["Raft", "Crash-Fault-Tolerant (CFT)", "Majority (f < n/2)", "Strong Leader (Leased)", "Kubernetes (etcd), CockroachDB"],
          ["Multi-Paxos", "Crash-Fault-Tolerant (CFT)", "Majority (f < n/2)", "Weak Leader / Proposer", "Google Chubby, Spanner Core"],
          ["PBFT", "Byzantine-Fault-Tolerant (BFT)", "Supermajority (f < n/3)", "Rotating Primary", "Permissioned Blockchain Networks"],
          ["ZAB", "Crash-Fault-Tolerant (CFT)", "Majority (f < n/2)", "Primary-Backup Leader", "Apache ZooKeeper Cluster Coordination"]
        ]}
      />

      <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 font-semibold mt-3">
        *Note: $n$ represents total cluster node count; $f$ is maximum simultaneous failed nodes tolerated without consensus stalling.
      </p>
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Walk through the mathematical quorum difference: CFT needs strictly > 50% majority (2f+1), whereas BFT needs > 66% supermajority (3f+1) to resist malicious collusion.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>

  <!-- Slide 5: Template D — Programming Construct with Verified Output & Pitfalls -->
  <Slide
    id="implementation"
    eyebrow="04 · PRACTICAL IMPLEMENTATION"
    title="HashRing Implementation in JavaScript"
    layout="code"
    unit="CS 4400: Distributed Systems"
  >
    <div class="my-auto">
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-900 font-medium mb-2">
        A functioning implementation of a consistent hash ring using virtual node multipliers and sorted key binary lookup:
      </p>

      <CodeBlock
        title="HashRing.js — Verified Node Router"
        lang="javascript"
        code={nodeHashingCode}
        output={"Assigned 'session-10492' to: node-alpha"}
        pitfalls={[
          "Failure to sort ring keys after node insertion leads to broken successor traversal.",
          "Using non-deterministic hash functions (like object references) causes inconsistent key lookup across cluster workers.",
          "Omitting the ring wrap-around boundary condition drops all keys greater than the highest node hash."
        ]}
      />
    </div>
    <SpeakerNotes>
      Point out line 14: sorting keys is mandatory for successor binary search. Explain the wrap-around logic on line 21.
    </SpeakerNotes>
  </Slide>
</DeckLayout>
